    16	
    17	# Goodhart risk: agents[] population metric becomes target when demanded
    18	
    19	## Context
    20	
    21	Risk identified during guardrails Ideation: if the project starts treating `agents[]` population rate as a metric, teams may game it by providing trivial or near-empty entries. The design does not add any gate that enforces quality of the populated fields — it only enforces presence.
    22	
    23	## Why deferred
    24	
    25	Deferred from the hook + reconstructor Ideation scope. The hook + reconstructor satisfy the literal ask (populate `agents[]`). Field-quality gates are a future extension.
    26	
    27	The hook + reconstructor session's success criterion was "≥ 90% population (12 fields × N entries)" — this is a presence threshold, not a quality threshold. Quality gates (e.g., non-null `step/phase/iter`) are downstream of field presence; they require a schema validator which was itself deferred as a separate work item.
    28	
    29	## When to pick up
    30	
    31	When the project decides to enforce `agents[]` field quality rather than mere presence — for example, when a schema validator for session.json is added.
    32	
    33	## Suggested approach
    34	
    35	- Add a schema validator gate that checks non-null values on critical `agents[]` fields (`step`, `phase`, `iter`).
    36	- Accept the risk for now: the presence gate is already useful and the Goodhart failure mode requires deliberate gaming.
    37	- A future session adding `agents[]` quality validation should reference this item as the motivating concern.
    38	
    39	## Originating session
    40	
    41	`.gobbi/projects/gobbi/sessions/` — originated in session 1b26cf20 guardrails Ideation.
    42	
    43	## Related
    44	
    45	- This risk was surfaced as a Risk-perspective evaluation finding during the guardrails Ideation (provenance recorded in that session's evaluation artifacts).
    46	- The schema-validator work this item depends on was itself deferred as a separate backlog item.

